{# ============================================================
   EXHIBIT NHBE-a  |  Defence Case File
   ============================================================ #}

{% set gridded = "<strong>NHBE-a-gridded</strong>" %}


<div class="exhibit-wrapper">

  {# ── EXHIBIT HEADER ─────────────────────────────────────── #}
  {{ exhibit_header([
      [["Exhibit Reference", "NHBE-a"],       ["Associated Gridded Reference", "NHBE-a-gridded"]],
      [["Subject Property",  FF],             ["Claimant / Neighbouring Holding", FFH]],
  ]) }}

  {# ── IMAGE OVERVIEW ─────────────────────────────────────── #}
  <div class="section">
    <h3>Image Overview</h3>

    {{ rotate_90 }}

    <p>
      An image taken in a broadly easterly direction up a track leading towards
      the concrete yard forming part of {{ FF }}.
      The image was taken from the edge of the access track to {{ FFH }},
      at a distance of approximately 50&nbsp;metres from the entrance to the property.
    </p>
    <p>
      For clarity, grid references as displayed on {{gridded}} may be used
      when identifying the items described below.
    </p>
  </div>

  {# ── EXHIBIT ITEMS ───────────────────────────────────────── #}
  <div class="exhibit-items-row">

    {# ── ITEM 1 — DELIVERY SIGN MATERIAL ───────────────────── #}
    <div class="section exhibit-item">
      <h3>Item 1 &mdash; Delivery Sign Material</h3>

      <table class="item-meta" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" border="1">
        <tr>
          <td width="30%"><strong>Grid Reference ({{gridded}})</strong></td>
          <td><i>H8&ndash;J9</i></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Location in Image</strong></td>
          <td>Bottom-left foreground</td>
        </tr>
      </table>

      <table class="item-section">
        <tr><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td>
          <p>
            Wooden material is visible in the bottom-left foreground of the image.
            Had the image been taken from a position several steps further back along
            the access track, this material would have been identifiable as part of a
            sign directing deliveries destined for {{ FF }}.
          </p>
        </td></tr>
      </table>

      <table class="item-section">
        <tr><th>Defence Response to Claims Made</th></tr>
        <tr><td>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Health and safety risk:</strong>
              It is not established how a delivery sign, functionally necessary to {{ FF }},
              constitutes a risk to the health and safety of the public.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nuisance:</strong>
              It has not been demonstrated how this item causes {{ FFH }} a nuisance.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Unsightliness:</strong>
              The item's functional requirement to {{ FF }} renders any aesthetic
              characterisation irrelevant; in any event the term &ldquo;unsightly&rdquo;
              is inherently subjective.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    {# ── ITEM 2 — RECLAIMED HARDWOOD TIMBER ────────────────── #}
    <div class="section exhibit-item">
      <h3>Item 2 &mdash; Reclaimed Hardwood Timber</h3>

      <table class="item-meta" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" border="1">
        <tr>
          <td width="30%"><strong>Grid Reference ({{gridded}})</strong></td>
          <td><i>F3&ndash;F4</i></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Location in Image</strong></td>
          <td>Approximately three-fifths down from the top, stored under the red dogwood bush</td>
        </tr>
      </table>

      <table class="item-section">
        <tr><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td>
          <p>
            A stack of small-section timber is visible at the far edge of the image.
            The timber is tropical hardwood, salvaged by carefully de-nailing pallets
            previously used to transport stone paving.
            The resulting batons are retained for general use on the holding.
            The hardwood's inherent resistance to rot makes outdoor storage unproblematic,
            and the timber is stored discretely beneath the red dogwood bush.
          </p>
          <p>
            The timber is located at the junction of two private tracks, both owned
            by {{ FF }}: the track over which {{ FFH }} holds no right of way, and
            the track over which {{ FFH }} does hold a right of way.
          </p>
        </td></tr>
      </table>

      <table class="item-section">
        <tr><th>Defence Response to Claims Made</th></tr>
        <tr><td>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Nuisance:</strong>
              The timber is at no point situated on the right of way enjoyed by {{ FFH }}.
              It is therefore difficult to understand in what manner it causes a nuisance,
              and a specific reasoned basis will be required if any such claim is to be sustained.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Unsightliness:</strong>
              The timber is located outside the boundaries of the {{ FFH }} title and is
              only visible from its right of way — a right of access, not a right to a view.
              The claim of unsightliness is further undermined by:
              <ol type="a">
                <li>the inherently subjective nature of the term;</li>
                <li>
                  the timber's status as potentially useful reclaimed material with a
                  further working life ahead of it; and
                </li>
                <li>
                  the fact that it falls outside the visual envelope of the {{ FFH }} title itself.
                </li>
              </ol>
              Had a specific and legitimate concern been raised regarding this item,
              {{ FF }} could have considered its relocation at its discretion; however:
              <ol type="i">
                <li>{{ FF }} is under no obligation to do so;</li>
                <li>
                  it appears that any visible location would be considered objectionable
                  by a representative of {{ NHB }}, making the standard imposed unreasonable; and
                </li>
                <li>
                  no compelling reason has been advanced as to why relocation to a less
                  convenient position would be necessary or proportionate.
                </li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Health and safety risk:</strong>
              It has not been demonstrated how stacked reclaimed timber constitutes a
              risk to the public.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Pre-existing status:</strong>
              The timber was in situ prior to {{ HNB }}'s purchase of {{ FFH }}.
              It is therefore presumed that any concerns regarding its status as a
              nuisance or health and safety risk would have been raised during conveyance,
              and {{ NHB }} should be in a position to provide evidence of this.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    {# ── ITEM 3 — WOODEN PALLET AND PLASTIC BARREL ─────────── #}
    <div class="section exhibit-item">
      <h3>Item 3 &mdash; Wooden Pallet and Plastic Barrel</h3>

      <table class="item-meta" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" border="1">
        <tr>
          <td width="30%"><strong>Grid Reference ({{gridded}})</strong></td>
          <td><i>F4&ndash;G5</i></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Location in Image</strong></td>
          <td>
            Approximately three-fifths down from the top and one-fifth in from
            the right; approximately 2&nbsp;metres from the edge of the right of way
            enjoyed by {{ FFHO }}
          </td>
        </tr>
      </table>

      <table class="item-section">
        <tr><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td>
          <p>
            A wooden pallet is leaned against an empty red 25-litre plastic barrel.
            The barrel is largely obscured by the pallet, which rests against it at
            an angle of approximately 70&deg; (from the recollection of {{ OH }} and
            {{ EH }}).
            Together these items serve an operational function, forming a barrier
            that restricts access down the track which forms the predominant subject
            of the image.
          </p>
          {# TODO: Confirm correct pallet designation before filing — "Euro-Pallet" or equivalent standard #}
        </td></tr>
      </table>

      <table class="item-section">
        <tr><th>Defence Response to Claims Made</th></tr>
        <tr><td>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Obstruction of access:</strong>
              The items are positioned approximately 2&nbsp;metres from the edge of
              {{ FFHO }}'s right of way and do not prevent lawful access along that route.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Health and safety risk:</strong>
              It is not clear how these items present a risk to public health and safety:
              <ol type="a">
                <li>
                  The pallet's position is stable and unlikely to become dislodged
                  in a manner capable of causing injury.
                </li>
                <li>
                  The pallet is of lightweight construction — notably lighter than
                  a standard Euro-Pallet — and would be unlikely to cause injury
                  were it to become dislodged.
                </li>
                <li>
                  The distance of approximately 2&nbsp;metres from the right of way
                  and bridleway is sufficient to prevent accidental interference
                  with the items.
                </li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nuisance:</strong>
              It is not apparent how these items constitute a nuisance.
              Once within the confines of {{ FFH }}, occupants would be unable
              to hear or smell them, nor do they impinge upon any right enjoyed
              by the owners or occupiers of {{ FFH }}.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Unsightliness:</strong>
              The items are not objectively unsightly: the red plastic of the barrel
              is obscured by the natural, faded wood colour of the pallet.
              This assessment is in any event subjective and does not alter the
              operational purpose the items serve.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    {# ── ITEM 4 — DARK BLUE MITSUBISHI VEHICLE ─────────────── #}
    <div class="section exhibit-item">
      <h3>Item 4 &mdash; Dark Blue Mitsubishi Vehicle</h3>

      <table class="item-meta" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="3" border="1">
        <tr>
          <td width="30%"><strong>Grid Reference ({{gridded}})</strong></td>
          <td><i>D5&ndash;E5</i></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Location in Image</strong></td>
          <td>
            Up and slightly left of Item 3; positioned at the entrance to the track
            forming the principal subject of the image
          </td>
        </tr>
      </table>

      <table class="item-section">
        <tr><th>Description</th></tr>
        <tr><td>
          <p>
            A dark blue Mitsubishi vehicle is positioned at the entrance to the track.
            The following facts are material to any assessment of this vehicle:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>The vehicle is fully operational and runs and drives in both high and low ratio gears.</li>
            <li>The vehicle's brakes are fully operational and are maintained as required.</li>
            <li>
              The vehicle previously rolled over during off-road use, resulting in:
              <ol type="a">
                <li>a damaged front windscreen;</li>
                <li>the dislodging and removal of some front cosmetic components; and</li>
                <li>the loss of the rear-left window.</li>
              </ol>
            </li>
            <li>
              The vehicle does not hold an MOT and is not taxed, as it is used exclusively
              on private property. It is inspected before each use.
            </li>
            <li>
              During the summer of 2025 the vehicle was vandalised: fuel was removed from
              the tank and the vehicle has since been awaiting repair at its current location
              at the entrance to the track. When vehicular access to the yard behind is
              required, the Mitsubishi is moved using another vehicle or tractor unit.
              <strong>The vehicle is not abandoned</strong>, as asserted by {{ NHB }}.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            When not in active use moving materials around {{ FF }}, the vehicle is
            routinely parked on the track that forms the main subject of NHBE-a,
            serving as a more substantial barrier restricting unauthorised access to
            the concrete yard to the rear.
          </p>
        </td></tr>
      </table>

      <table class="item-section">
        <tr><th>Defence Response to Claims Made</th></tr>
        <tr><td>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Health and safety risk:</strong>
              It has not been demonstrated how the vehicle presents a health and
              safety risk to the public. It is operational, inspected before use,
              and stored on private land not visible from the {{ FFH }} title.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Nuisance:</strong>
              It has not been demonstrated in what capacity the vehicle constitutes
              a nuisance.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Unsightliness / Abandonment:</strong>
              The vehicle is an operational asset stored in a location not visible
              from the {{ FFH }} title. Its cosmetic condition following an off-road
              incident is not a relevant consideration for {{ NHB }}, and the
              assertion that it has been abandoned is factually incorrect.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

  </div>{# ── end exhibit-items-row ───────────────────────────── #}

</div>
